{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"08","chapter_title":"Data Storytelling With Excel","title":"Module 3 Data Story Studio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 3 data story studio to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel","cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/03/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_calibration"]},"content":"# Module 3 Data Story Studio\n\n![Module 3 Data Story Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-08-module-3-data-story-studio.png)\n\nThis is the capstone of the module, where the separate skills become one pipeline. Cleaning, formulas, pivots, charts, ethics, and narration are not five unrelated lessons; they are stages of a single workflow that turns a raw export into a data story a reader can trust. The studio asks you to run that whole pipeline end to end on one dataset and to show that every number ties back to the original rows.\n\nThe skill that only appears at the full-pipeline level is reconciliation: making sure the headline number, the pivot cell, and the chart bar all agree, because each stage can introduce a mismatch the previous stage hid. A clean sheet can still feed a stale pivot; an honest pivot can still feed a misleading chart; an accurate chart can still get a wrong headline. The data story is finished only when the chain holds from raw row to published claim.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou have a raw store export of six orders. By the end of this studio you will hand a reader a single slide: a claim-first headline, an honest bar chart, a source note, and a small-sample caveat. But between the export and the slide are five decisions, and a slip at any one of them quietly corrupts the slide while leaving it looking polished.\n\nSuppose your headline says West led at 2,400 but the pivot row for West reads 1,200. The slide looks finished; the chain is broken. That mismatch is almost always a stale pivot range or an active filter, and a reader who re-traces your numbers will catch it even though the chart is beautifully formatted. The studio is where you learn to catch it first, by reconciling the headline against the pivot against the bar before anything ships.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe data-story pipeline has five stages, each drawing on one earlier section, plus a reconciliation gate at the end:\n\n| Stage | Draws on | What it produces | Failure it prevents |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clean | 03.01 | A tidy table, consistent labels, numeric values | Split categories, skipped numbers |\n| Summarize | 03.04 | A pivot with the right aggregation | Answering the wrong question |\n| Chart | 03.03 | An honest figure for the real question | A misleading axis or chart type |\n| Narrate | 03.07 | A claim-first, faithful headline | Overstated or buried findings |\n| Protect | 03.06 | A sample-size caveat where n is small | Over-reading a tiny dataset |\n\nThe reconciliation gate sits across all five. Its rule is simple and strict: the number in your headline must equal the matching pivot cell, which must equal the height of the matching chart bar. If West is 1,200 in the pivot, the West bar must read 1,200 and the headline must say 1,200. Three independent representations of the same fact, forced to agree, is what makes a data story checkable. When they disagree, the disagreement points to the exact stage that failed, usually a stale pivot range or a filter left on.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThis capstone chains every skill from Module 3 into one verifiable data story. Start from a raw export of six rows.\n\n```\n   A       B       C\n1  Region  Order   Sales\n2  west    1001    400\n3  East    1002    250\n4  West    1003    600\n5  south   1004    150\n6  East    1005    300\n7  West    1006    200\n```\n\nStep 1 (clean). Standardize Region case so `west`/`West` and `south`/`South` are consistent: West, East, South. Confirm Sales are numbers, not text.\n\nStep 2 (summarize). Build a pivot with Region in Rows and Sum of Sales in Values: West = 1200, East = 550, South = 150. Grand Total = 1900.\n\nStep 3 (chart). The question is \"which region led?\", a category comparison, so use a bar chart with the value axis starting at 0. West's bar is more than double East's, matching 1200 vs 550.\n\nStep 4 (narrate). Write a claim-first headline tied to the numbers: \"West led Q1 sales at 1,200, more than double East (550); source: store export, 6 orders.\" Note the small n so no one over-reads it. (An assistant such as Copilot in Excel, or a general-purpose chat assistant, can critique this draft headline against your own pivot numbers; as of 2026 tool names and behavior change quickly, so record which tool you used and verify its critique against the pivot yourself.)\n\nStep 5 (reconcile). The headline number (1,200 for West) must equal the West row in the pivot and the height of the West bar. If the headline said 2,400, that mismatch means a stale pivot range or a filter, and you fix it before publishing.\n\nThe auditable package: cleaned data, pivot summary, honest chart, claim-first headline, source note, and a reconciliation check. A reviewer can re-trace every number from the six raw rows.\n\nRun the six-order export through the pipeline one stage at a time, predicting each move before it is revealed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Chain the full pipeline on the six-order export\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The Region column holds west, West, south, and South. Before any pivot or chart, what is the first stage?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Build the bar chart so you can see the shape\",\"Clean: standardize Region case and confirm Sales are numeric\",\"Write the headline from the raw rows\",\"Average the sales across all six orders\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Clean first. Standardize case so west/West and south/South merge into West, East, South, and confirm Sales are numbers, not text. Every later stage assumes this one is correct.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The question is which region led. What aggregation and chart fit?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Average of Sales, shown as a line chart over time\",\"Sum of Sales by Region, shown as a bar chart with the axis at 0\",\"Count of orders, shown as a pie chart\",\"Sum of Sales, shown with the axis starting at 1000\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A category comparison wants Sum of Sales by Region (West 1200, East 550, South 150) on a bar chart with a zero baseline, so bar length is honest.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Your headline reads West led at 2,400 but the West pivot row shows 1,200. What does the mismatch mean?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The chart is just rounding differently\",\"A stale pivot range or a leftover filter; fix it before publishing\",\"Both numbers are fine; pick whichever looks better\",\"The raw data must be wrong, so delete a row\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The three-way match failed. Headline, pivot cell, and bar must all read 1,200. A 2,400-vs-1,200 gap almost always means a stale pivot range or an active filter, so reconcile before anything ships.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The finished story: cleaned regions, Sum-by-Region pivot, zero-baseline bar chart, the claim-first headline \\\"West led Q1 sales at 1,200, more than double East (550); source: store export, 6 orders,\\\" and a passing three-way match traceable to the raw rows.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the five pipeline stages plus the reconciliation gate as they must run, since each stage assumes the one before it is correct.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Clean the raw export (consistent labels, numeric values)\",\"Summarize with a pivot using the right aggregation\",\"Chart the real question with an honest axis and chart type\",\"Narrate a claim-first, faithful headline with a source note\",\"Protect the reader with a sample-size caveat when n is small\",\"Reconcile: confirm headline equals pivot cell equals chart bar before sharing\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun the five stages in order and do not publish until the reconciliation gate passes. Cleaning before summarizing, summarizing before charting, and reconciling before sharing is not bureaucracy; each stage assumes the one before it is correct.\n\nBefore the data story ships, confirm the chain end to end:\n\n1. **Trace-back check:** Can every number in the headline be re-derived from the raw rows by re-running clean and summarize?\n2. **Three-way match check:** Does the headline number equal the pivot cell and the chart bar for the same category?\n3. **Caveat check:** Does the story name its source and, when n is small, say so plainly?\n\nIf the headline, the pivot, and the bar do not all show the same figure, stop and find which stage drifted before you do anything else.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common capstone error is polishing the slide before reconciling the numbers. A crisp chart with a confident headline can still carry a 2,400-versus-1,200 mismatch from a stale pivot, and formatting effort spent before the three-way match just makes a wrong story look more credible. Reconcile first, format last.\n\nA second misreading is assuming that because each stage worked alone, the pipeline works together. The pivot may have been correct yesterday, but if you cleaned a new batch of rows this morning without refreshing, the chart now disagrees with the data. Integration introduces failures no single stage could, which is exactly why the reconciliation gate exists.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your slide is polished: the West bar is clearly tallest and the headline reads West led at 2,400. But the West row in the pivot reads 1,200. What should you conclude?\" explanation=\"The three-way match failed: headline, pivot cell, and bar must all show the same figure. A 2,400-vs-1,200 gap signals a stale pivot range or a filter left on, not a finished story. Polish never substitutes for reconciliation, so fix the chain before the slide ships.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The slide is done; the polish shows the work is careful\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The reconciliation gate failed; a stale range or filter broke the chain, so fix it before publishing\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Use 2,400 since the headline is the most visible number\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRun the full pipeline on a different raw export: a semester of help-desk tickets with columns Building, Date, and Minutes-to-resolve, mixed case and a few durations typed as text. The question is \"which building's tickets took longest on average?\"\n\nClean first: standardize Building case so `Library` and `library` merge, and strip the `min` suffix from text durations so Minutes is numeric. Summarize next: a pivot with Building in Rows and Minutes in Values, aggregation set to Average, not Sum, because the question is about typical resolution time. Chart third: a bar chart with the axis at 0, since this is a category comparison, so the longest building's bar is honestly proportional. Narrate fourth: \"Science Hall tickets took longest to resolve, averaging 47 minutes versus 22 in the Library (one semester, 60 tickets).\" Protect: 60 tickets is modest and one semester, so note it is not a permanent ranking.\n\nNow the reconciliation gate. The headline 47 minutes must equal Science Hall's pivot row and the height of its bar. If the bar reads 47 but the headline says 52, you mistyped the headline; if the pivot reads 38, your chart is built on a stale range. Only when all three agree, traceable back to the cleaned tickets, is the data story done.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of a finished data story works backward from the headline to the raw data, checking that the chain holds at every link. The audit path:\n\n1. Take the headline number and find it in the chart, then in the pivot.\n2. From the pivot cell, identify which source rows feed it and re-sum or re-average them by hand.\n3. Confirm the source note and sample size match what the pivot actually covers.\n\nWhen you can take any published claim and walk it back to the original rows without the author's help, you are reading the pipeline's integrity, not just its surface.\n\nThis trace-back discipline ties the capstone pipeline to the module concepts of evidence-based revision and bounded AI support. Map how the stages relate.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong capstone artifact exposes the whole chain and an explicit reconciliation step, not just the polished slide. The trace should list the cleaning fixes, the pivot aggregation, the chart choice, the headline, the caveat, and the three-way match. A model line reads: \"Cleaned region case and stripped text from Sales; pivot Sum by region gives West 1,200; the West bar reads 1,200 and the headline says 1,200; source is the store export of six orders, a small sample, so I flagged it.\"\n\nThe weak version submits a clean-looking slide and asserts the numbers are right. The strong version lets a reviewer re-derive the headline from the raw rows and see the headline, pivot, and bar agreeing, so the entire story is checkable from export to claim.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same pipeline-with-reconciliation transfers to any tool that turns records into a summarized claim, including the dashboard tools in later modules. Move the help-desk data into a reporting dashboard and the stages persist: you still clean the source, define an aggregation, choose a visual, write a headline, and note the sample, and you still must confirm that the dashboard's big number equals the underlying query equals the displayed chart. The interface changed from Excel to a dashboard, but the integrity discipline, every number traceable to source and three representations forced to agree, is what carries over, which is the real product of this module.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-08-module-3-data-story-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.08. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"],"stem":"You are assembling a one-page data story from a raw export. Which order of steps produces a trustworthy result?","options":["Build the chart first, then clean the data, then write the headline.","Clean the data, summarize it with a pivot, choose the right chart, then write a claim-first headline tied to the numbers.","Write the headline you hope for, then find data that fits it.","Polish the colors first, then decide what the story is."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible data story flows from clean data to summary to chart to headline. Building visuals or writing the headline before cleaning the data risks telling a story the numbers do not support.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_chart_before_clean","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio"],"stem":"Your finished data story shows a chart whose total (1,900) does not match the headline number (2,400). Before publishing, what is the right move?","options":["Publish anyway because the chart looks finished.","Trace the mismatch: the headline likely counted rows the pivot range excluded, so reconcile the two before publishing.","Change the headline to a vaguer claim so no one checks.","Delete the chart and keep only the headline."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The headline and the chart must come from the same reconciled numbers. A mismatch means a step in the pipeline (range, filter, or stale pivot) is out of sync and must be fixed first.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_unreconciled_numbers","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"For the capstone data story, what is a responsible use of an AI assistant?","options":["Have the AI invent the dataset and the finding for you.","Ask the AI to critique your draft headline against your own numbers, then accept or reject each suggestion yourself.","Paste your classmates' raw personal data so the AI can label rows.","Let the AI pick a chart type without telling it what question you are answering."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The capstone keeps the learner responsible for the data and the claim. AI can critique a draft against your real numbers, but it should not fabricate findings or receive others' personal data.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_ai_makes_the_story","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_3_data_story_studio","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which final data story package lets a reviewer fully verify your work?","options":["A single chart image with no numbers or source.","The cleaned data, the pivot summary, the chart, the claim-first headline, the data source, and a note on what you excluded and why.","Only the headline sentence.","A long list of every formula keystroke with no result."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable data story exposes the whole chain: clean data, summary, chart, headline, source, and exclusion notes. The reviewer can then re-trace each step rather than trust a lone image.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_8_module_3_data_story_studio_chart_before_clean","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"447916cd2a57af7812bec32bcefbe29ac62b248ac2711c135dd0c7ffd3adad8b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}